Id: no-wall-clock
Pattern: \b(DateTime\.(Now|UtcNow|Today)|DateTimeOffset\.(Now|UtcNow)|Environment\.TickCount|Stopwatch|TimeProvider\.System)
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template/Time/SystemClock.cs
Issue: 47

Half of what this plugin does is time: a cache that expires, a retention limit,
a refresh cadence, a backoff, a rate window. A read of the wall clock where it
stands makes every one of those testable only by sleeping, and a test that
sleeps is a test that gets deleted.

The exception is the one implementation of IClock that reads the machine, which
#47 added, and it is written as that file's path.

IT WAS A FILENAME SUFFIX UNTIL #387, AND THE REASON WRITTEN HERE FOR THE SUFFIX
WAS WRONG IN THE DIRECTION THAT DECIDED IT. It said a full path would turn #14's
rename of the project directory into a silently disabled invariant. The rename is
loud rather than silent, and it is loud twice. An exception naming a path that no
longer exists excludes nothing, so the file it was written for is back inside the
subject and leg 3 refuses it, and leg 6 refuses the exception itself for naming
no tracked file. Both were watched by pointing this exception at the path the
rename would produce and running the lint. What the suffix bought instead is what
#387 is about: any file anywhere in the tree whose name ended SystemClock.cs was
outside this invariant, whatever it did, and the person adding that file chose
the name.

The exception is one file and widening it is how this rule stops meaning
anything. A second reader of the machine's clock belongs behind IClock, not
beside SystemClock.

TWO SPELLINGS OF THE SAME READ ARE HERE AND THE FIRST FOUR DID NOT REACH THEM,
and both are what somebody measuring an elapsed interval writes first rather
than something exotic. A Stopwatch is the machine's monotonic clock read where
it stands, and it is the obvious tool for a timeout and for a backoff, which are
two of the five things listed above; nothing about it says DateTime, so a rule
written as the four named properties was silent on it. TimeProvider.System is
the runtime's own answer to the question IClock already answers here, and taking
it would put a second source of the current time in front of the injected one
while every existing pattern stayed quiet.

The type name carries the whole of the Stopwatch arm rather than its members,
because StartNew, GetTimestamp and GetElapsedTime are three ways to the same
read and a pattern naming them is a pattern that goes quiet when a fourth
arrives. TimeProvider is matched only where it names the system instance: a
provider handed to a caller is an injected seam of the kind this rule exists to
push work towards, and refusing the type outright would refuse that too.

Its bound is the one every rule here has. A time read reached by reflection, or
assembled from a string at run time, is invisible to a pattern that reads one
line at a time, and an await on something that reads the clock somewhere else
leaves nothing on the line that asks for it.
